<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Despotate of Angelokastron and Lepanto</h1><p class="st-meta">1358 CE – 1408 CE (approx.) · capital Angelokastron</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Albanian principality in Aetolia under the Shpata family, holding Angelokastron and the port of Lepanto after the Serbian conquest of Epirus.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-ii-orsini">Nikephoros II Orsini</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> was killed by Albanian clans in 1359, Albanian leaders divided southern Epirus. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjin-bua-shpata">Gjin Bua Shpata</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angelokastron">Angelokastron</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafpaktos">Lepanto</a> (Naupaktos) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolia">Aetolia</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-losha">Peter Losha</a> held the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/despotate-of-arta">Despotate of Arta</a>. Both received the title of despot from the Serbian ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simeon-uros">Simeon Uroš</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shpata-family">Shpata</a> ruled Albanian clans who had migrated south as herders and soldiers, alongside a Greek population. They fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-anjou">Angevins</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tocco-family">Tocco of Kefalonia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navarrese-company">Navarrese Company</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. In 1374 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjin-bua-shpata">Gjin Bua Shpata</a> also took Arta, joining the two Albanian despotates.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjin-bua-shpata">Gjin</a>'s death in 1399 his family held on briefly: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafpaktos">Lepanto</a> was sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1407, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angelokastron">Angelokastron</a> fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlo-i-tocco">Carlo I Tocco</a> around 1408.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/despotate-of-angelokastron-and-lepanto.jpg" alt="Despotate of Angelokastron and Lepanto" width="320" height="261"><figcaption>Image: Ahmet Q. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Despotate_of_Arta,_1390.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1358 CE – 1408 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Angelokastron</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gjin Bua Shpata, Paul Shpata</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Albanian; Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of Angelokastron</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1383&amp;focus=despotate-of-angelokastron-and-lepanto" aria-label="Despotate of Angelokastron and Lepanto on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5217 755 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Despotate of Angelokastron and Lepanto lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5519 1030h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5537 1011h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5537 1011h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Greece. <a href="/atlas?year=1383&amp;focus=despotate-of-angelokastron-and-lepanto">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
